    <!-- Direction / limit-switch discrepancy (H). Latched when a valve has been driven for 5s
         and the OPPOSITE limit switch is still made — i.e. the physical evidence contradicts the
         command. Covers two causes the PLC cannot tell apart from two limit switches: the valve
         moved the wrong way (OPEN/CLOSE swapped at the interposing relays, a commissioning fault)
         and the valve never left its seat (dead actuator / stuck limit switch). Both need the same
         operator response - go look at the valve - so they share one alarm rather than splitting
         into two the operator would treat identically. Found 2026-08-15 by live reversal test:
         previously a valve commanded CLOSE whose OPEN limit made would display a settled green
         "FULLY OPEN" for the full 8s travel timeout, then raise a misleading Fail-to-Close. -->
    <Member Name="DirFault" Datatype="Array[1..89] of Bool" Remanence="NonRetain" Accessibility="Public">
      <AttributeList>
        <BooleanAttribute Name="ExternalAccessible" SystemDefined="true">true</BooleanAttribute>
        <BooleanAttribute Name="ExternalVisible" SystemDefined="true">true</BooleanAttribute>
        <BooleanAttribute Name="ExternalWritable" SystemDefined="true">true</BooleanAttribute>
      </AttributeList>
    </Member>
    <!-- Last commanded position: 0 = none since startup, 1 = OPEN, 2 = CLOSE. Set when a command
         is accepted, never cleared on arrival - it is the operator's intent, not a live state, so
         the HMI can show COMMANDED vs ACTUAL whenever the two disagree. That comparison is what
         catches a stuck limit switch (valve reports a position it is not in), which is the more
         likely in-service fault than wrong-direction movement. -->
    <!-- Position-only state, 3=OPEN 4=CLOSED 5=NO POSITION 6=OPENING 7=CLOSING, 0=unconfigured.
         Identical to StateCode EXCEPT that a fault never overrides it. StateCode answers "is this
         valve alright"; PosCode answers "where is it". Both are needed at once: a faulted ballast
         valve sitting open is a flooding path and a faulted one sitting closed is a blocked line,
         and the operator could not tell them apart when FAULT replaced the position on screen. -->
    <Member Name="PosCode" Datatype="Array[1..89] of Int" Remanence="NonRetain" Accessibility="Public">
      <AttributeList>
        <BooleanAttribute Name="ExternalAccessible" SystemDefined="true">true</BooleanAttribute>
        <BooleanAttribute Name="ExternalVisible" SystemDefined="true">true</BooleanAttribute>
        <BooleanAttribute Name="ExternalWritable" SystemDefined="true">true</BooleanAttribute>
      </AttributeList>
    </Member>
    <!-- Which fault, most specific first: 0=none 1=unhealthy 2=double indication 3=fail to open
         4=fail to close 5=loss of position 6=unexpected movement 7=direction/limit fault.
         Lets the popup name the cause instead of just saying FAULT. -->
    <Member Name="FaultCode" Datatype="Array[1..89] of Int" Remanence="NonRetain" Accessibility="Public">
      <AttributeList>
        <BooleanAttribute Name="ExternalAccessible" SystemDefined="true">true</BooleanAttribute>
